+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/CASI> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Siltstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DIGS> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Sandy siltstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DUNCL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Locally nodular mudstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DUNSH> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Locally nodular mudstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/FLOS> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Siltstone and mudstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/GVIN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Carbonaceous siltstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/KILDN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Dominated by silty fissile mudstones intercalated with shell beds, shelly limestones, bone beds and one sandstone bed (Emeleus, 1997, appendix 1). The biota comprises bivalves, conchostracans, gastropods and plants (ferns), shark teeth and plesiosaur bones. There is a prominent bed of stromatolitic algal limestone, 0.20 m thick, with gypsum pseudomorphs at the top of the member recognisable throughout the Hebrides Basin."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LONFN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Comprises dark grey-brown finely laminated fissile mudstone ('paper shale') with interbedded ooidal limestone. Brackish water faunas, including bivalves, conchostracans, gastropods and ostracods are present. Desiccation cracks, indicating emergence, are present in the uppermost beds."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TCN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Silty mudstone."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/BOU> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Rests non-sequentially on olive grey bioturbated mudstone of Frome Clay Formation."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/CASA> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Scaladal Sandstone Member (SCSA)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/CASI> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Camasunary Sandstone Member (CASA)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DIGS> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Glashvin Silt Member (GVIN)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DUNCL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Dunans Shale Member (DUNSH)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/DUNSH> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Abruptly overlies the fine- to medium-grained sandstone of the Belemnite Sands Member (BSSK), Staffin Bay Formation (STBA)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/FLOS> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Digg Siltstone Member (DIGS)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/GVIN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Dunans Clay Member (DUNCL)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/KILDN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "As for parent formation."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LONFN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Kildonnan Member (KILDN)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/SCSA> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "Underlain by the Tobar Ceann Siltstone Member (TCN)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TCN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "A sharply-defined and significant upward facies change from the medium- to coarse-grained sandstone of the Carn Mor Sandstone Member (CMSA) of the Staffin Bay Formation (STBA) to the silty mudstone of the Tobar Ceann Siltstone Member (Sykes, 1975, fig. 6)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/SCSA> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasOldestAge> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JC> .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TCN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasOldestAge> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JC> .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/SCSA> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasOldestAgeValue> "165.3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TCN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasOldestAgeValue> "165.3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> .
